                <pb facs="0404" n="322" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg322"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 895.00/517.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Ambassador to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1910-10-14"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">October 14, 1910</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1239.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The answer of the minister for foreign
                    affairs to my note (No. 491) to him of the 20th ultimo, was received on the 6th
                    instant, and I beg to transmit a copy herewith. This reply is not sufficiently
                    definite in its promises, especially as regards the seventh clause of my note.
                    Without waiting for further instructions and considering that the desire of the
                    Department is already understood, I <pb facs="0405" n="323" xml:id="pg323"/>
                    have ventured as of this date to write a further note (No. 502) to the minister
                    for foreign affairs, a copy of which will be found herewith. * * *</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. J. O’Brien.</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d418-a1.xml" xpointer="d418-a1"/>
